  "notes": "'This house is planned with studied irregularity, with the view of obtaining the greatest amount of external picturesque effect.' The tower here earns its keep differently from the one on page 474: it holds the staircase. 'The tower is a prominent feature, and is not, as is too often the case, a comparatively useless adjunct, but serves to contain an open and well lighted staircase, which, in general, should be its use in houses of this description; in the roof the cistern for water supply is placed.' Every door is stated in the prose. The coal cellar and the water closet 'are placed so as to be accessible without entering the Kitchen department', which is the same reasoning as the lobby under the stairs in the other villa. 1500 pounds.",
